I could not verify this claim, and I am not refuting it either. supported=false only because I saw no evidence. None of my tools reached a source.

1. WebSearch for "Big Companies Are Starting to Hire Again" WSJ AI: no search ran. The tool said this turn's 200-call budget, which all agents share, was used up. As the tool told me, I did not try to get around the limit with other search engines or proxy services.
2. WebFetch failed on all three cited URLs with "getaddrinfo ENOTFOUND": politicalwire.com/2026/07/27/big-companies-are-starting-to-hire-again/, view.asiae.co.kr/en/article/2026072715174336892, and the ibtimes.com article.
3. WebFetch on www.wsj.com: "Claude Code is unable to fetch from www.wsj.com".
4. curl from Bash on the same three URLs: "CONNECT tunnel failed, response 403". The network policy blocks these hosts.
5. A search of /home/user/GregoryDich for "hire again", "hiring again", "Sarah Franklin" and "more benign" found nothing, so no cached copy of the evidence is stored in the repo.

What is still unverified:
- that the WSJ article exists, and its exact headline and publication date. The claim's own caveat says it may be dated 2026-07-26 US time rather than 2026-07-27;
- the five named companies (CSX, Alphabet, ServiceNow, Booz Allen Hamilton, Snap-on);
- the Sarah Franklin (Lattice) quote;
- the Robert Half CEO quote 'more benign than some have feared';
- the description of the hiring as 'modest and targeted'.

The claim admits its own evidence was 'search-result summaries only', and I could not raise that standard. Recommendation: re-run this check in a session that has WebSearch budget left, or have someone open a WSJ-syndicated copy (Political Wire or IBTimes) by hand and confirm the date and quotes before the event is coded. Files changed: none.

I could not check this claim. That is not a refutation. I saw no source for or against it, so supported=false here only means "not verified".

Checks run and what they returned:
1. WebSearch for "Newsom signs SB 951 Cal/WARN technology displacement AI layoffs" was not performed. The tool replied: "this turn's web search budget is used up (limit: 200 WebSearch calls per turn, shared by every agent in it)". It also said not to work around the limit through other search engines, readers, proxies or archives, so I did not.
2. WebFetch failed with "getaddrinfo ENOTFOUND <host>" on all eight URLs I tried. These were the seven cited sources (forbes.com, news.bloomberglaw.com, natlawreview.com, littler.com, fisherphillips.com, omm.com, cdflaborlaw.com) plus the official bill-status page on leginfo.legislature.ca.gov (bill_id=202520260SB951).
3. curl through the session proxy to leginfo.legislature.ca.gov and forbes.com gave "CONNECT tunnel failed, response 403", meaning the network policy blocks them. The proxy status page lists recent connect_rejected (403) entries, natlawreview.com among them.
4. A search of the local repo /home/user/GregoryDich for "SB 951", "SB951" and "technology displacement" found nothing.

Not verified, and to check in a later round when a search budget is available:
- that Newsom signed SB 951 on 2026-09-30, and that the 2027-01-01 effective date is right;
- the "in substantial part" trigger, the "technology displacement" statement at the top of the notice, and the required contents of the notice;
- the EDD summaries and report to the legislature;
- that the 60-day notice period, coverage thresholds and penalties are unchanged;
- that SB 947 was signed the same day and takes effect 2027-07-01;
- the Forbes piece dated 2026-10-05 and its "drawing new lines around AI at work" framing.

The best primary source is the leginfo bill-status/history page for SB 951 and SB 947, which gives the Governor's approval date and chapter number. Even if signing on 2026-09-30 is confirmed, the event date falls inside the 2026-06-10..2026-10-10 window.

No files were changed.

I fetched the primary page https://www.anthropic.com/research/economic-index-june-2026-report myself with WebFetch, twice. What it showed:
- Date "Jun 26, 2026" and title "Anthropic Economic Index report: Cadences". The date is inside the 2026-06-10..2026-10-10 window.
- The survey was launched in April 2026, with a linked sample of about 9,700 respondents.
- "Cadences" covers usage rhythms: the workweek, daily and hourly patterns, and tax-deadline spikes. Hourly time of day is inferred from the state of the IP address.
- Confirmed verbatim:
  - early-career workers report the highest share of their work doable by AI and the most concern about job loss;
  - respondents are more worried about job loss for others than for themselves;
  - over one third put the chance of a junior colleague losing their job next year above 60%;
  - 10% rate losing their own job as likely or very likely, 38% of those attribute that forecast to AI, and the authors call this an upper bound;
  - people with 15+ years of experience put the share of tasks AI can do about 10 pp lower;
  - the heaviest delegators to Claude are the most optimistic, with the largest effects on expected pay and on ability to find a job;
  - the survey is "not representative of the general population";
  - the page labels the answers "self-assessments" (in the skills context).
- The "over half" sentence uses "and/or", so the summary's "and expect new industries" overstates it slightly (see corrections).

Three things could not be checked:
- The secondary source www.explainx.ai would not resolve (DNS ENOTFOUND).
- WebSearch was refused because the per-turn search budget (200, shared across agents) was used up, so I did not check media coverage or other secondary reports. That is not a refutation.
- The summary's "first from an AI lab" and "about 40%" claims are not on the primary page and should be dropped.

Direction "mixed" is justified: the job-loss worry is concentrated among early-career workers, while delegators are optimistic and respondents hope for collaboration or new jobs. The publisher, Anthropic, is an AI vendor, and the note already says so. No repository files were changed and no local checks were run; this task was a web fact-check only.
